<$APEXPROOT/sys/src/cmd/mksyslib

CC=pcc
LD=pcc
CFLAGS= -c -C

# THE LAST TWO OFILES ARE OURS AND THE REST ARE EDG'S, which is why
# they sit after the generated list rather than in alphabetical order
# among it: `cxa_atexit.c' and `dso_handle.c' live in THIS directory
# and every other name resolves under `$EDGSRC'.  EDG declares
# `__cxa_atexit'/`__cxa_finalize' in `include_c++/cxxabi.h' and
# defines neither, leaving them to the platform's C++ runtime -- so
# the first link of `cmd/edg' answered
#
#	_Z12__call_dtorsv: undefined: __cxa_finalize
#
# and that is a gap EDG leaves open rather than one this tree opened.
# Their own headers say the rest.
#
# AND THEY ARE COMPILED WITHOUT `-C', which is the point of the
# separate rule rather than an accident of writing one.  `-C' marks
# every TEXT and GLOBL in the translation unit dupok, which is right
# for generated C where a duplicate is the same entity by
# construction and wrong for a file somebody wrote: it would turn a
# second definition of `__cxa_atexit' -- the exact collision these
# two are placed to avoid -- into first-wins silence.

%.$O: $EDGSRC/%.c
	$CC $CFLAGS $EDGSRC/$stem.c

# `main.c' IS RENAMED, AND IT IS WHY cpfe CRASHED ON EVERY RUN.
# `lib_src/main.c' defines `void _main(void)' -- EDG's static-init
# helper, the one a target's own crt is expected to call -- and
# **`_main' IS `6l''s DEFAULT ENTRY POINT**: `6l/obj.c:305' is
# `INITENTRY = "_main"', and `sys/src/ape/lib/ap/arch/amd64/main9.s:3'
# is `TEXT _main(SB), 1, $0'.  `6l' creates that name as an SXREF at
# startup and satisfies it from the FIRST archive on the link line
# that defines it -- `libedg.a', which `cmd/edg/mkfile' names ahead of
# libap.  So the program's entry became EDG's helper: it ran
# `_Z12__call_ctorsv' (which returns at once -- `__head' is null in
# `vars.c:49' and `munch_ctors.c' defines `_ctors[1] = {0}') and then
# executed a plain `RET'.
#
# **AND THE FAULTING ADDRESS IS `argc'.**  `main9.s' reads the kernel's
# argument block at `0(SP)', so `0(SP)' at entry holds argc -- which is
# exactly what that `RET' popped and jumped to.  `cpfe --help' is
# **argc 2** and answered
#
#	cpfe 78213: suicide: sys: trap: fault read addr=0x2 pc=0x2
#
# *A faulting pc equal to the argument count is not a coincidence that
# could have arisen any other way*, and it is checkable without a
# rebuild: `cpfe' alone must have said `pc=0x1' and `cpfe a b c'
# `pc=0x4'.
#
# NOTHING HID IT AND `-C' IS NOT IMPLICATED, which was worth checking
# before blaming the blunt flag standing next to it.  There was no
# duplicate to silence: `main9.$O' is pulled ONLY to satisfy the entry
# symbol (its other global, `_tos', is referenced from `profile.c'
# alone), so with `libedg.a' answering first that member never
# reached the link at all.  First-archive-wins, no diagnostic
# available from any linker.
#
# RENAMED RATHER THAN DROPPED FROM OFILES, and the choice is this
# tree's own rule about capabilities.  Nothing anywhere references
# `_main' -- the sweep below is complete -- so removing the member
# would link just as well and would throw away the only entry point
# into EDG's static-initialisation machinery.  `__edg_main' keeps it,
# in the reserved double-underscore space, inside an archive and not a
# public header, so the `reject' hazard does not apply.
#
# **THE STATIC-INIT GAP IS REAL AND IS RECORDED, NOT FIXED**: with
# `_main' no longer the entry, NOTHING CALLS `_Z12__call_ctorsv', so a
# C++ program cpfe translates would not run its static constructors.
# That costs cpfe nothing (it is translated C++ with no dynamic
# initialisation that reaches this path -- it runs, which is the
# measurement) and it is the next thing to answer for translated
# programs.  cpfe does not emit a call to `_main' either: the name
# occurs in the whole 133-file corpus exactly twice, both in
# `lib_src/main.c', with no string literal of it in the emitter -- so
# upstream's convention here is a crt that calls it, and this target's
# crt (`main9.s' -> `_callmain' -> `_apemain' -> `main') does not.
#
# AND THE SWEEP IS WHAT SAYS `_main' IS ALONE.  All 51 files compiled
# with gcc and `nm --defined-only -g' taken over the objects gives
# exactly FOUR external definitions that are neither `_Z'-mangled nor
# `__'-prefixed: `_ctors', `_dtors', `_main' and `_new_handler'.  The
# other three collide with nothing in libap or kencc.  *`cmd/edg/mkfile'
# records that "every DEFINITION under lib_src is `_Z'-mangled or
# `__'-prefixed" -- that is wrong, and it is the sentence that would
# have found this.*  Asking the linker's own answer costs one command
# where reading 51 files costs a round.
# **AND THE `-D` IS QUOTED, WHICH IS NOT DECORATION.** mk hands a
# recipe to `rc`, and in rc `=` is a SYNTAX CHARACTER -- legal only as
# an assignment at the start of a command or inside quotes -- so a bare
# `-D_main=__edg_main` is a parse error rather than an argument:
#
#	/rc/lib/rcmain:24 *eval*:1: token '=': syntax error
#	mk: pcc -c -C ...  : exit status=rc 79720: rc 79720: exit
#
# *The message names rcmain and a token, and nothing in it is about
# this mkfile.*  Every other `-D` in the tree arrives through an
# expanded variable (`$CFLAGS`), where mk substitutes after rc has
# parsed, so this is the first literal one here -- which is why a rule
# this file's own CLAUDE.md records (`awk -v name=$name` is a parse
# error, `'name='^$name` is not) had no precedent to copy in the
# directory.
main.$O: $EDGSRC/main.c
	$CC $CFLAGS '-D_main=__edg_main' $EDGSRC/main.c

cxa_atexit.$O: cxa_atexit.c
	$CC -c cxa_atexit.c

dso_handle.$O: dso_handle.c
	$CC -c dso_handle.c

clean:V:
	rm -f *.[$OS]

nuke:V:
	rm -f $LIB
